# Data protection

## Storage tiers (choose deliberately)

| Data class | Storage | Examples |
|---|---|---|
| Secrets & credentials | **Keychain (iOS) / Keystore (Android)** via `react-native-keychain` or equivalent | Refresh tokens, biometric-gated secrets |
| Sensitive user data | Encrypted storage (e.g. MMKV with encryption key held in Keychain/Keystore) | Session data, personal profile cache |
| Non-sensitive preferences | AsyncStorage / plain MMKV | Theme, locale, onboarding flags |

**Forbidden:**

- Tokens, passwords, or PII in plain AsyncStorage, `localStorage` (web), or files in the
  app sandbox without encryption.
- Encryption keys hard-coded in JS or derived from static app constants — keys live in the
  platform keystore.
- Hand-rolled cryptography. Use platform crypto through vetted libraries; standard
  algorithms only (AES-256-GCM for symmetric encryption, SHA-256+ for hashing,
  platform PBKDF/Argon2 for key derivation).

## PII handling

- Classify fields when designing a store: secret / sensitive / non-sensitive. The store doc
  records the classification.
- `partialize` persisted store state so sensitive fields are excluded or routed to the
  encrypted tier.
- On logout: wipe all sensitive tiers (Keychain entries, encrypted stores, in-memory
  state). Logout is a data-destruction event, not just a navigation event.
- Honor data-deletion requests end-to-end: local wipe + server-side deletion call.

## Logging and telemetry

- **Never log** tokens, credentials, PII, or full API payloads — in `console.*`, crash
  reporters, or analytics.
- Crash-reporting breadcrumbs carry identifiers (IDs, screen names), never contents.
- `console.log` statements are stripped from release builds (Babel transform); crash
  reporters are the release-channel telemetry.

## Screen-level protection

- Screens rendering sensitive data (financial, health, credentials) enable the platform
  screenshot/recording protection where the product requires it, and mask content in the
  app switcher snapshot.
- Sensitive fields use `secureTextEntry` and disable keyboard autofill/suggestions where
  inappropriate (`autoComplete="off"`, `importantForAutofill="no"` case by case).
- Clipboard: never auto-copy sensitive values; when copy is a feature, prefer expiring
  clipboard APIs where available.

## Backups

- Exclude sensitive files/preferences from OS backups (`android:allowBackup="false"` or a
  scoped backup ruleset on Android; appropriate Keychain accessibility classes on iOS —
  `kSecAttrAccessibleWhenUnlockedThisDeviceOnly` for device-bound secrets).
